Principles: SDD

Purpose:
  Define the architecture principles the AI applies when analyzing,
  designing, implementing and reviewing project code. This is the
  global rules layer.

Owns:
  Architecture principles
  Architecture boundaries
  Modularity rules
  Dependency rules
  Layering rules
  Coupling rules
  Service extraction rules
  Architecture validation

DoesNotOwn:
  Project-specific architecture
  .sdd's own component graph (see architecture.sdd)

CorePrinciple:
  Code MUST be designed so that a modular monolith can be separated
  into independently deployable services without requiring a complete
  rewrite.

ArchitectureGoals:
  modularity, low coupling, high cohesion, explicit boundaries,
  dependency direction, replaceability, testability, observability,
  scalability, service extraction readiness

Rules:

  [AP1]  Every business domain MUST have an explicit boundary.
  [AP2]  Domain boundaries MUST NOT depend on implementation paths.
  [AP3]  Cross-domain communication MUST use explicit contracts.
  [AP4]  Internal implementation details MUST NOT leak across boundaries.
  [AP5]  Dependencies MUST point toward stable abstractions.
  [AP6]  Circular dependencies MUST NOT exist.
  [AP7]  A module MUST have a clearly defined responsibility.
  [AP8]  Shared code MUST NOT become an uncontrolled dependency hub.
  [AP9]  Business logic MUST NOT depend directly on infrastructure.
  [AP10] External integrations MUST have explicit boundaries.
  [AP11] Database access MUST remain inside the responsible boundary.
  [AP12] A domain MUST NOT access another domain's internal database.
  [AP13] Cross-domain data access MUST use an approved contract.
  [AP14] Domain logic MUST remain independently testable.
  [AP15] A module SHOULD be independently replaceable.
  [AP16] A module SHOULD be independently deployable in the future.
  [AP17] Architecture MUST NOT introduce microservice complexity
          without an actual requirement.
  [AP18] Architecture selection MUST be proportional to project scope,
          team, budget, and operational constraints.

DependencyDirection:
  Presentation > Application > Domain > Infrastructure

DomainInteraction:
  Domain A > Contract > Domain B

Forbidden:
  Domain A > Domain B internal
  Domain A > Domain B database
  Presentation > Infrastructure directly

ModuleBoundary:
  Every module MUST define:
    purpose, owns, exposes, depends_on, forbids

ServiceExtraction:
  A module is extraction-ready when:
    boundary is explicit, dependencies are explicit, contracts are explicit,
    internal state is isolated, database ownership is known,
    external integrations are isolated, tests are independent,
    cross-module communication is explicit

  ExtractionTarget:
    ModularMonolith > IsolatedModule > ExplicitContract >
    IndependentDataOwnership > ServiceCandidate > Microservice

Important:
  Service extraction readiness does NOT mean "build microservices now".
  It means "do not design the monolith in a way that makes future
  extraction unnecessarily expensive."

ArchitectureSelection:
  Criteria:
    project scope, domain complexity, expected growth,
    scalability requirements, team constraints, operational requirements,
    security requirements, integration requirements, cost

  Principle:
    Prefer the simplest architecture that satisfies the criteria.
    Avoid speculative complexity not justified by current requirements.

  Examples:
    small scope, low complexity       -> modular application
    medium scope, moderate complexity  -> modular monolith
    large scope, high complexity       -> modular monolith, or distributed
                                        when criteria justify it
    independently-scalable components -> service extraction MAY be
                                        considered

ArchitectureValidation:
  Every feature MUST be checked for:
    boundary violation, dependency violation, circular dependency,
    coupling, cohesion, data ownership, contract ownership,
    infrastructure leakage, scalability risk, extraction risk

ArchitectureFailure:
  If architecture validation fails, implementation MUST NOT be
  considered complete.

  Flow:
    implementation > architecture-review > FAILED > recover > review > VR

Resolution:
  Global architecture rules (this file)
    > Project architecture (project/architecture.sdd)
    > Domain architecture (project/<domain>/architecture.sdd)
    > Feature architecture
    > Implementation

Navigation:
  Architecture: @architecture.sdd
  Levels: @levels.sdd
  Patterns: @patterns.sdd

